    "https://apol-recruit.ucsd.edu/JPF04672": {
      "title": "Executive Director for the Master of Professional Accountancy Program (JPF04672)",
      "description": "Executive Director for the Master of Professional Accountancy Program (JPF04672) - UC San Diego AP On-Line Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Executive Director for the Master of Professional Accountancy Program (JPF04672) Executive Director for the Master of Professional Accountancy Program Apply now to Executive Director for the Master of Professional Accountancy Program Job #JPF04672 RADY SCHOOL OF MANAGEMENT / Rady School of Management / UC San Diego Position overview Position title: Executive Director for the Master of Professional Accountancy Program Salary range: A reasonable salary range estimate for this position is $175-275k. ​The posted UC academic salary scales ( 2026-27 academic salary scales ) set the minimum pay determined by rank and/or step at appointment. \" Off-scale salaries”, i.e., a salary that is higher than the published system-wide salary at the designated rank and step, are offered when necessary to meet competitive conditions See the following table for the salary scale for this position [ table 34 ] Application Window Open date: October 5, 2026 Next review date: Sunday, Oct 25, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Sunday, Jan 3, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The Rady School of Management’s Master of Professional Accountancy (MPAc) program is a self-supporting program that admitted its first cohort of 33 students in 2019 and has steadily built up to an annual cohort of around 80 students. The MPAc program provides rigorous, hands-on training to prepare students to work as accounting professionals. Through a curriculum composed of quantitative, analytical, legal and regulatory coursework, graduates of the program will be prepared to lead accounting, auditing, tax, and consulting initiatives for accounting firms, corporations, small businesses, non-profit organizations, governments, and individuals. The Academic Administrator / Executive Director will provide administrative oversight for all operational and curricular development activities for the MPAc Program. Preparing our students for employment as professional accountants requires intimate knowledge of the profession, including the current accounting skills and quantitative profile that our students must develop to be competitive in the marketplace. An effective Executive Director will possess a unique blend of academic expertise, familiarity with industry practice, and broad network of industry contacts - particularly with accounting firms - in order to facilitate graduate employment outcomes and liaise with local and global companies. By bringing together academic excellence, knowledge of industry demand profiles, and industry contacts at the highest level, the Executive Director will ensure that we attract the very best students, achieve outstanding employment outcomes, and further add to the program’s reputation. The Executive Director reports to and serves as principal advisor to the Associate Dean for Academic Programs on all administrative, operational, and/or curricular matters relating to the MPAc program. Primary responsibilities include: Administration of program planning and development: development of proposals for extramural funding of campus programs, assessment of program and constituency needs, implementation of innovative program changes, evaluation of program activities and functions, creativity and originality in program development and usage of resources, supervision and leadership of staff, serving as a liaison with other agencies and institutions in the public and private sectors, academic advising, co-curricular programs, supervision, course assessment activities, and an extensive amount of logistics surrounding the program. This position also includes teaching duties in accordance with the Program's needs. Interaction with other functional areas: such as Marketing, Recruitment /Admissions, Financial Aid, Student Programs and Services, Career Services and Corporate Relations. This position is also responsible for ensuring the highest levels of student satisfaction and for developing and maintaining effective, streamlined channels of communication among and for students, faculty and program staff. Continuous Program improvement: organize and serve on internal ad hoc and scheduled reviews. Reviews will occur in response to regular feedback from students, alumni and employers, as well as corporate/industry involvement, events and expectation analysis. The program director will coordinate the required AACSB Assurance of Learning activities and in response to assessment results, consider the appropriateness and effectiveness of the curriculum for achieving program objectives and learning goals. Program : https://rady.ucsd.edu/programs/master-professional-accountancy/ Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) At least 10 years of experience in the accounting world providing high-level administrative management, including planning, leadership, and analysis. Terminal degree in Accounting, Finance, or Economics, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience with teaching or development of graduate-level courses. Preferred qualifications Masters of Professional Accountancy, PhD, or equivalent degree in Accounting, Finance, or Economics. An ability to network with national and international business and academic contacts. Experience with academic degree programs. An ability to  to successfully address the educational and academic needs of the student population,  including reviewing, developing and updating curriculum and instructional strategies to ensure successful outcomes for all students. Excellent communication skills and cross-cultural abilities to maximize effective collaboration with a diverse community of campus and external colleagues. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter Statement of Teaching Statement of Research (Optional) Teaching Evaluations (Optional) Misc / Additional (Optional) Reference requirements 3-5 required (contact information only) Please provide contact information.  Letters will be solicited for final candidate and will not be checked without prior notification. Apply link: https://apol-recruit.ucsd.edu/JPF04672 Help contact: AP@rady.ucsd.edu About UC San Diego The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a university employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time. Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California prohibits smoking and tobacco use at all of its university-controlled properties. The UC San Diego Annual Security & Fire Safety Report is available online at: https://www.police.ucsd.edu/docs/annualclery.pdf . This report provides crime and fire statistics, as well as institutional policy statements & procedures. Contact the UC San Diego Police Department at (858) 534-4361 if you want to obtain paper copies of this report. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct, are currently being investigated for misconduct, left a position during an investigation for alleged misconduct, or have filed an appeal with a previous employer. Click for more information on Assembly Bill-810 Click for more information on Senate Bill-791 • “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. • UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy: [ https://policy.ucop.edu/doc/4000385/SVSH ] • UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties: [ https://policy.ucop.edu/doc/1001004/Anti-Discrimination ] Job location San Diego, CA Apply now to Executive Director for the Master of Professional Accountancy Program or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, San Diego is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, San Diego is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports AP On-Line Recruit University of California, San Diego Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://apol-recruit.ucsd.edu/JPF04574": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate or Full Project Scientist- Earth Section (JPF04574)",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate or Full Project Scientist- Earth Section (JPF04574) - UC San Diego AP On-Line Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Assistant, Associate or Full Project Scientist- Earth Section (JPF04574) Assistant, Associate or Full Project Scientist- Earth Section Apply now to Assistant, Associate or Full Project Scientist- Earth Section Job #JPF04574 IGPP, GEOSCIENCES RESEARCH DIV. / Scripps Institution of Oceanography / UC San Diego Position overview Position title: Project Scientist (FY) - Assistant, Associate, or Full Salary range: A reasonable salary range estimate for this position is $83,700 - $101,100. “Off-scale salaries”, i.e. a salary that is higher than the published system-wide salary at the designated rank and step, are offered when necessary to meet competitive conditions. Posted UC academic salary scales: Current Academic Salary Scales | UCOP ] set the minimum pay determined by rank and/or step at appointment.] [ Represented Project Scientist salary scale ] Application Window Open date: October 8, 2026 Next review date: Monday, Nov 9, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Friday, Oct 8, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The Earth Section within the Scripps Institution of Oceanography (SIO) at UC San Diego is committed to academic excellence within the faculty, staff and student body, and invites applications from qualified individuals for an Assistant, Associate, or Full Project Scientist for an open search. The Earth Section consists of the Geosciences Research Division and the Institute of Geophysics and Planetary Physics. The Earth Section within the Scripps Institution of Oceanography at the University of California, San Diego is conducting an open search for Project Scientist positions (non-tenured, Assistant, Associate or Full level). UC San Diego is seeking experienced scientists in geochemistry, geophysics, geodesy or a related field to carry out both independent and collaborative research projects. Examples of research projects include but are not limited to: sediment core analysis, radiocarbon and OSL sediment dating, numerical modeling of earthquake processes, seismic structural imaging, and natural hazards mitigation using seismogeodesy. Under supervision of the Principal Investigators, the Project Scientists will function independently, and the role includes: 1) designing, managing and conducting experiments and research; 2) supporting their Principle Investigator with the preparation and submission of proposals to funding agencies; 3) leading and making innovative and significant contributions to lab project(s); 4) participating and presenting at scientific meetings; 5) writing scientific reports and manuscripts for submission to top-tier peer-reviewed journals. ​​ At UC San Diego, Project Scientists are academic researchers who are expected to make significant and creative contributions to a research team, but are not necessarily leaders of the research team. They are not required to carry out independent research or develop an independent research reputation, but will publish and carry out research or creative programs with supervision by a member of the Professor or Professional Research series. University and public service are encouraged but not required, and they do not have formal teaching responsibilities. Appointments and duration vary depending on the length of the research project and availability of funding. Appointment at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Project Scientist level will be based on the candidate's background and experience. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled based on the on-going needs of Earth Section. A link to the full description of the series is provided for your review: Project Scientist See the Department websites: Geosciences Research Division website and Institute of Geophysics and Planetary Physics website Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) Ph.D. or nearing graduation in the field of Geochemistry, Geophysics, Earth and Ocean Science, Physics, Geodesy, Engineering, or related field. Experience with quantitative data analysis,data management skills, familiarity with computer operating systems. Strong organizational and communication skills to report analysis and research findings in an academic setting. Proven record of peer-reviewed publications. Additional qualifications (required at time of start) Ph.D. in the field of Geochemistry, Geophysics, Earth and Ocean Science, Physics, Engineering, or related field. Preferred qualifications For Geochemistry track: Extensive experience with advanced laboratory techniques such as chrono-bio-chemo-stratigraphy and geophysical interpretation methods. For Geophysics track: Proficiency in numerical tool and methods development, application and testing, participation in geophysical field work. For Geodesy track: Experience in geodetic methods, working in a research group. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter (Optional) Statement of Research Statement of Teaching (Optional) List of Publications -   Include a current list of publications Misc/Optional (Optional) Reference requirements 3-5 required (contact information only) Apply link: https://apol-recruit.ucsd.edu/JPF04574 Help contact: srhoten@ucsd.edu About UC San Diego The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. As a university employee, you will be required to comply with all applicable University policies and/or collective bargaining agreements, as may be amended from time to time. Federal, state, or local government directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California prohibits smoking and tobacco use at all of its university-controlled properties. The UC San Diego Annual Security & Fire Safety Report is available online at: https://www.police.ucsd.edu/docs/annualclery.pdf . This report provides crime and fire statistics, as well as institutional policy statements & procedures. Contact the UC San Diego Police Department at (858) 534-4361 if you want to obtain paper copies of this report. As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct, are currently being investigated for misconduct, left a position during an investigation for alleged misconduct, or have filed an appeal with a previous employer. Click for more information on Assembly Bill-810 Click for more information on Senate Bill-791 • “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment, discrimination, dishonesty, or unethical conduct, as defined by the employer. • UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy: [ https://policy.ucop.edu/doc/4000385/SVSH ] • UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, Students and Third Parties: [ https://policy.ucop.edu/doc/1001004/Anti-Discrimination ] Job location San Diego, CA Apply now to Assistant, Associate or Full Project Scientist- Earth Section or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California, San Diego is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California, San Diego is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports AP On-Line Recruit University of California, San Diego Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
